sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch04-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Where organisms compete most closely, as on a small patch of ground, the advantages of diversification mean that the coexisting inhabitants generally belong to different genera and orders.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The closest-competing neighbours tend to belong to different genera and orders";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IV, “Natural Selection”; section “Divergence of Character, related to the diversity of inhabitants of any small area, and to naturalisation”. Scope: small-area communities, e.g. turf plots, islets, small ponds"
}
